---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionHeading from "../components/SectionHeading.astro";
import { work } from "../data/content";
import { withBase } from "../utils/paths";
---
<BaseLayout title="Engineering Multiplier" description="How Kavish Agarwal scales architecture through mentoring, reusable patterns, governance, and technical leadership.">
  <section class="page-intro section-pad section-dark"><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Leadership</p><h1>Engineering Multiplier</h1><p class="lede">The highest level of technical leadership is not measured only by what one person can build, but by how much stronger the surrounding organization becomes.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section-pad"><div class="container leadership-grid"><div class="pull-quote">“The goal is not to become the person who must solve every hard problem. The goal is to raise the capability of the organization so more people can solve hard problems well.”</div><div class="prose"><p>My leadership philosophy is practical: develop technical leaders, create ownership and visibility, turn learning into reusable capability, stay hands-on without becoming the bottleneck, and scale through communities rather than hierarchy alone.</p><p>I coach engineers through architecture tradeoffs rather than simply providing answers. I use design reviews, architecture office hours, reference patterns, ADRs, inner sourcing, and open technical conversations so good decisions travel across organizational boundaries.</p><p>Staying senior does not mean stopping implementation. I step into code, debugging, production issues, benchmarks, or prototypes when risk is high—then transfer the learning back to the team. The question is always where personal implementation is the highest-leverage action and where enabling others creates more scale.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section-pad section-tint"><div class="container"><SectionHeading eyebrow="In practice" title="Leadership shows up in the seams." /><div class="leadership-practices"><article><span>01</span><h3>Develop technical leaders</h3><p>Help engineers learn how to defend decisions, understand tradeoffs, and own outcomes.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Create ownership and visibility</h3><p>Give people meaningful responsibility and include them in important design conversations.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Turn learning into capability</h3><p>Convert discoveries from code, incidents, and prototypes into patterns, reviews, and decision records.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Scale through communities</h3><p>Use cross-team forums, inner sourcing, and open design reviews so good decisions spread.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section-pad"><div class="container"><SectionHeading eyebrow="A case in point" title="Governance as enablement." intro="Architecture practices work best when they increase organizational throughput rather than becoming a bottleneck." /><div class="feature-strip"><div><p class="eyebrow">Selected work</p><h3>{work[3].title}</h3></div><a class="text-link" href={withBase(`/work/${work[3].slug}`)}>Read the case study <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
